course: books
lesson: 78
title: Just So Stories (Rudyard Kipling), 4: How the Leopard got his Spots, part 2 (One, two, three, and where's your breakfast?)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Leopard and the hunter wait for dark, and each grabs something he can smell and feel but not see; they sit on their catches all night, and in the morning they find a Giraffe now covered in blotches and a Zebra now covered in stripes.
explain: The Zebra and the Giraffe show off their trick: they step into the striped and blotchy shadows, count "One--two--three!", and vanish, laughing "where's your breakfast?"
explain: The two hunters see they no longer match their background, and at last the Leopard understands Baviaan's riddle: not other spots in South Africa, but spots on his skin.
explain: The hunter presses his five fingertips all over the Leopard, leaving little marks in groups, and now the Leopard can look like pebbles, rocks or sunlight through leaves; the old saying "Can the leopard change his spots?" comes from here, says Kipling.
explain: Parts of this story use old words and ideas about people that are racist and wrong; we leave those out and read the animal story, which is about camouflage, matching where you live.
fact: The Leopard grumbled, "This daylight hunting is a perfect scandal", so they "waited till dark", and the Leopard jumped at something that "smelt like Zebra, and it felt like Zebra, and when he knocked it down it kicked like Zebra, but he couldn't see it".
fact: He called it "O you person without any form" (without a shape you can see) and sat on its head till morning; the hunter did the same with something that kicked "like Giraffe".
fact: At "bright morning-time" the Leopard asked, "What have you at your end of the table, Brother?", joking that their catches were their breakfast.
fact: The Giraffe was "covered all over with chestnut blotches" and the Zebra "covered all over with black and purple stripes"; "fulvous" and "tawny" are old words for a golden yellow-brown.
fact: The Leopard says, "if you were on the High Veldt I could see you ten miles off", and the Zebra answers, "but this isn't the High Veldt. Can't you see?"
fact: The Zebra went to "little thorn-bushes where the sunlight fell all stripy", the Giraffe to "tallish trees where the shadows fell all blotchy", and they said "One--two--three! And where's your breakfast?"
fact: The hunter sums it up: "The long and the little of it is that we don't match our backgrounds" ("the long and the little of it" = the whole truth in short).
fact: Parts of this story use old words and ideas about people that are racist and wrong; we do not repeat them.
fact: The Leopard thought Baviaan meant places: "I went into other spots as fast as I could ... and a lot of good it has done me", but the hunter explains, "He meant spots on your skin."
fact: The Leopard did not want stripes ("I wouldn't look like Zebra--not for ever so") nor spots too "vulgar-big" ("I wouldn't look like Giraffe"); "not for ever so" = not for anything.
fact: The hunter "put his five fingers close together" and pressed them all over the Leopard, and "wherever the five fingers touched they left five little black marks, all close together".
fact: "Now you are a beauty!": on bare ground the Leopard can "look like a heap of pebbles", on naked rocks "a piece of pudding-stone" (a rock full of round pebbles), on a leafy branch "sunshine sifting through the leaves", and right across the middle of a path "nothing in particular. Think of that and purr!"
fact: The old saying "a leopard cannot change its spots" means that people do not change what they are really like; Kipling jokes that it only exists because the Leopard did it once.
fact: Real biology: a leopard's markings are "rosettes", rose-shaped rings of dark spots with a lighter middle; they are not prints of five fingers, and they are in the leopard's genes from birth.
fact: Real biology: every leopard has its own pattern, like a fingerprint, so scientists can tell leopards apart on hidden cameras; even black leopards ("black panthers") have faint rosettes.
fact: Real biology: leopards hunt mostly at night by creeping close and pouncing, and often drag their prey up a tree; scientists still argue why zebras have stripes, and a strong idea is that stripes keep biting flies from landing.
exercise: When did the Leopard and the hunter decide to hunt? | After dark | At noon | At breakfast | At five o'clock
exercise: What did the Leopard say about daylight hunting? | "This daylight hunting is a perfect scandal" | "Daylight hunting is the very best of all" | "We must hunt only while the sun shines" | "Daylight is when the Zebra is easiest seen"
exercise: How did the Leopard know his catch was a Zebra? | It smelt, felt and kicked like Zebra | He could see its black and purple stripes | Baviaan told him it was a Zebra | It told him its name was Zebra
exercise: What did "O you person without any form" mean? | You thing whose shape I cannot see | You thing that has never been to school | You thing with no manners at all | You thing that cannot stand up
exercise: What did the Leopard and the hunter do with their catches all night? | Sat on their heads till morning | Tied them up with their braces | Ate them in the dark at once | Took them to Baviaan to ask
exercise: What did the Leopard mean by "What have you at your end of the table"? | What animal have you caught for breakfast | Which seat would you like at the dinner table | Have you laid the plates and spoons for tea | Is there any cake left at your end of it
exercise: What was the Giraffe covered with in the morning? | Chestnut blotches | Black and purple stripes | Five-finger spots | Wavy grey lines
exercise: What was the Zebra covered with? | Black and purple stripes | Chestnut blotches | Sandy-yellow fur | Little round rosettes
exercise: From how far could the Leopard have seen the Zebra on the High Veldt? | Ten miles off | One mile off | Fifty North | Seventeen feet
exercise: Where did the Zebra go to show his trick? | Little thorn-bushes where the sunlight fell all stripy | Tallish trees where the shadows fell all blotchy | A pool of water where he could see his reflection | The top of a palm tree where nobody could reach him
exercise: Where did the Giraffe go? | Tallish trees where the shadows fell all blotchy | Little thorn-bushes where the sunlight fell all stripy | Back out onto the bare, hot, shiny High Veldt | A great grey-green, greasy river with fever-trees
exercise: What did the Zebra and the Giraffe say before they vanished? | "One--two--three! And where's your breakfast?" | "Humph! Humph! Humph! And now you can't see us!" | "Them that takes cakes makes dreadful mistakes!" | "Come hither, Little One, and I will whisper!"
exercise: What did the hunter decide was wrong with them both? | They did not match their backgrounds | They were far too slow to catch anything | They had eaten too many rats and beetles | They had forgotten what Zebra looked like
exercise: What did "the long and the little of it" mean? | In short, the whole truth | A very long and boring story | A tall man and a short man | The longest and smallest animals
exercise: Where did the Leopard think Baviaan's "other spots" meant? | Other places in South Africa | Spots of mud on the riverbank | Spots on the Giraffe's coat | Other baboons in the forest
exercise: What did Baviaan really mean by "spots"? | Spots on the Leopard's skin | Places in the forest | Spots of light in the trees | Specks of dust in the desert
exercise: Why did the Leopard not choose stripes? | He did not want to look like Zebra | Stripes would make him look too thin | Baviaan had said that stripes were rude | The hunter could not draw straight lines
exercise: What did the Leopard ask for his spots? | Not to make them too big | To make them as big as he could | To make them purple and gold | To make them only on his tail
exercise: How did the hunter make the spots? | Pressed his five fingertips all over the Leopard | Painted them on with a stick dipped in mud | Threw pebbles at him until he was spotty | Stood him in the speckled shadows for a year
exercise: Which is NOT one of the things the spotted Leopard could look like? | A ripe banana in a smokehouse | A heap of pebbles | A piece of pudding-stone | Sunshine sifting through leaves
exercise: What is "pudding-stone"? | A rock with round pebbles set in it | A very hard old Christmas pudding | A stone used for baking big cakes | A soft grey rock that melts in rain
exercise: What does the saying "a leopard cannot change its spots" mean today? | People do not change what they are really like | Leopards cannot see well in the dark forest | Spotty animals should never paint themselves | People should not move to other places
exercise: Real biology: what are a real leopard's markings called? | Rosettes | Blotches | Fingerprints | Stripes
exercise: Real biology: how can scientists tell one leopard from another? | Each leopard has its own pattern of rosettes | Every leopard has exactly five spots per group | Leopards wear numbers on collars from birth | Only the black leopards have any patterns
exercise: Real biology: what is a strong scientific idea about zebra stripes? | They help keep biting flies away | They grew from standing in stripy shade | They were painted by the first hunters | They show how old each zebra is
exercise: Thinking back to part 1: who is Quite the Wisest Animal in All South Africa? | Baviaan the Baboon | The Djinn of All Deserts | The small 'Stute Fish | The Leopard himself
exercise: Thinking back to part 1: why had the animals gone to the forest? | To get away from the Leopard and the hunter | To find better grass after the dry season | Because Baviaan had told them to change | Because the Giraffe wanted to eat leaves
exercise: Thinking back to the Rhinoceros story: how many buttons did the Rhino's skin have? | Three | Two | Five | Four
test: Ruby says: "The Leopard got spots cos he stood in the shade like the Giraffe did." Put her right in the story. | In the story the hunter pressed his fingertips on him | In the story Baviaan painted them on with mud at night | In the story he rolled in the speckled pebbles of a river | In the story the Zebra kicked spots into him by mistake
test: Zayn, London: "Zebra just went 'one, two, three' and dipped, bare jokes." ("dipped" = left quickly, "bare" = very, a lot of.) Where did he hide? | Among little thorn-bushes in stripy sunlight | Behind a big rock on the open High Veldt | Under the water of a pool in the forest | Up a tall tree where the shadows were blotchy
test: What would have happened if the Leopard had chosen stripes? | He would have looked like the Zebra, which he did not want | He would have stayed sandy-yellow because stripes wash off | Baviaan would have come and taken his stripes back again | He would have turned into a zebra and been hunted himself
test: Grandma: "The Leopard was so cross, wasn't he, when he got his spots?" Put her right. | No, he was excited, and the hunter told him to purr | Yes, he growled at the hunter and ran off to sulk | Yes, he tried to rub them off against a palm tree | No, he did not notice them until much later on
test: Aussie uncle: "Leopard and Zebra, sittin' on 'em all arvo? Nah mate, all night." ("arvo" = afternoon.) Till when did they sit on them? | Till bright morning-time | Till five o'clock | Till lunchtime | Till the next week
test: Josie, Gen Z: "Baviaan's riddle was lowkey genius ngl." ("lowkey" = quietly, "ngl" = not gonna lie.) Why was it so clever? | "Spots" meant both places and marks on the skin | "Spots" was the name of a hidden forest town | "Spots" was a secret password for the Zebra | "Spots" meant the spots of rain on the veldt
test: True or false: real leopards have groups of exactly five spots, made by fingertips. | False: they have rosettes, and they are born with them | True: you can count five in every group on any leopard | True: but only on leopards that live in forests | False: real leopards have stripes, not spots at all
test: Mum, cross: "Where's your breakfast? I'll tell you where, it's in the bin cos you were late." Which animals in the story teased the hunters with "where's your breakfast?" | The Zebra and the Giraffe | The Eland and the Koodoo | Baviaan and the Ostrich | The Djinn and the Camel
test: The Camel liked looking at his own reflection in a pool. What was the Leopard proud of at the end? | His new spots that let him hide anywhere | His long legs that let him run so fast | His sandy coat that matched the High Veldt | His loud bark like Baviaan the Baboon's
test: In the Rhinoceros story and in this one, an animal gets marks on his skin for ever. Which is the right pair? | The Rhino got folds from rubbing; the Leopard got spots from fingertips | The Rhino got spots from fingertips; the Leopard got folds from rubbing | The Rhino got stripes from shadows; the Leopard got a hump from magic | The Rhino got blotches from the sun; the Leopard got buttons from a Parsee
test: In the Whale story the clever helper was a little fish. Who helps the Leopard by explaining the riddle in the end? | The hunter | The Zebra | The Giraffe | Baviaan himself
test: Unlike the Whale, the Camel and the Rhino, the Leopard is not punished. How does his change come? | He asks for it, to hunt better | A Djinn gives it for laziness | He gets it for stealing a cake | A sailor jams it in his throat
test: Sam, Scouse: "Leopard's spots are boss, la, he can lie on a path an' look like nothin'." ("boss" = great, "la" = mate.) What does the hunter say he can look like on a path? | Nothing in particular | A heap of pebbles | A piece of pudding-stone | Sunshine through leaves
test: ★★★ The hunter's fingers make "five little black marks" each time. If he pressed his hand on the Leopard ten times and the fingers never slipped, how many marks would there be? | Fifty | Fifteen | Ten | Five hundred
test: ★★★ Read carefully: Zebra says "this isn't the High Veldt. Can't you see?" and the Leopard says "I can now ... But I couldn't all yesterday." Why can he see the Zebra now? | It is morning and he is sitting right on him | The Zebra has turned plain sandy-yellow again | The forest shadows have all gone away for ever | Baviaan has given the Leopard special new eyes
test: ★★★ The Leopard did not want to look like Zebra, nor like Giraffe with "vulgar-big" spots. Which pattern did that leave him? | Small spots close together | Long black stripes | Big chestnut blotches | No marks at all
exam: Identify the means by which the Leopard and the hunter ascertained the identity of the animals captured during the night. | By smell, by touch and by the manner of their kicking | By sight, the starlight being sufficient for the purpose | By the testimony of Baviaan, who attended the capture | By the animals' own declaration of their names aloud
exam: Ascertain the principal deficiency which, in the hunter's own estimation, impeded the success of both hunters in the forest. | They did not match their backgrounds | They had forgotten the shapes of the game | They lacked sufficient bows and arrows | They were hunting at the wrong time of year
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the intended meaning of Baviaan's counsel to "go into other spots". | To acquire spots upon the skin | To depart for other regions of South Africa | To seek another wise animal for advice | To hunt only in the hours of darkness
exam: State, in accordance with established zoology, the correct designation of the markings upon the coat of a leopard. | Rosettes, ring-shaped clusters of dark spots | Fingerprints, groups of exactly five marks | Blotches, large patches of chestnut colour | Stripes, long bands of black and purple
